1
00:00:07,984 --> 00:00:11,737
I, Claire Hale Underwood,
do solemnly swear

2
00:00:11,738 --> 00:00:14,769
that I will faithfully
execute the office

3
00:00:14,770 --> 00:00:16,700
of President of the United States...

4
00:00:16,701 --> 00:00:20,037
I'm proud of her. She deserves this.

5
00:00:20,038 --> 00:00:23,248
Even if it only lasts
for a couple of weeks.

6
00:00:23,249 --> 00:00:27,420
And will, to the best of my ability,
preserve, protect, and defend

7
00:00:27,421 --> 00:00:30,732
the Constitution of the
United States, so help me God.

8
00:00:34,886 --> 00:00:38,322
Congratulations, Madam Acting President.

9
00:02:16,264 --> 00:02:22,075
<font color="#007700">Synced and corrected by Aaronnmb</font>
www.addic7ed.com

10
00:02:23,327 --> 00:02:25,913
- Feel different?
- No.

11
00:02:25,914 --> 00:02:29,725
- Liar.
- It's temporary. I'm only acting.

12
00:02:30,794 --> 00:02:32,937
Oh.

13
00:02:35,214 --> 00:02:38,776
You have to now carry
this with you at all times.

14
00:02:40,386 --> 00:02:44,490
- The codes?
- Mmm-hmm. It's the law.

15
00:02:54,776 --> 00:02:56,068
Governor, we just got word

16
00:02:56,069 --> 00:02:59,029
that Vice President Claire Underwood
was sworn in as acting president.

17
00:02:59,030 --> 00:03:01,031
Were you aware?

18
00:03:01,032 --> 00:03:02,550
Of course.

19
00:03:03,827 --> 00:03:05,636
Do you have any comment?

20
00:03:06,663 --> 00:03:07,830
God bless America.

21
00:03:07,831 --> 00:03:09,832
If Monday's House vote goes your way,

22
00:03:09,833 --> 00:03:12,668
- and you win the presidency...
- Oh, I'll win, believe me.

23
00:03:12,669 --> 00:03:14,086
You think this vote will be the charm?

24
00:03:14,087 --> 00:03:16,797
See, Congress is no different
than the rest of the country.

25
00:03:16,798 --> 00:03:21,176
People are sick and tired of
all this confusion and chaos.

26
00:03:21,177 --> 00:03:23,637
The public is not happy.

27
00:03:23,638 --> 00:03:25,318
If you become president,

28
00:03:25,319 --> 00:03:27,766
would Claire Underwood
remain as vice president?

29
00:03:27,767 --> 00:03:30,310
Not if she wants what's
best for the country.

30
00:03:30,311 --> 00:03:33,747
But given the Twelfth Amendment,
would you have a choice?

31
00:03:34,816 --> 00:03:35,983
Uh, no further questions, everybody.

32
00:03:35,984 --> 00:03:38,026
The governor's here to
talk to members of Congress.

33
00:03:38,027 --> 00:03:39,695
Thank you so much.

34
00:03:39,696 --> 00:03:41,989
Thank you. Thanks, everybody.

35
00:03:41,990 --> 00:03:44,008
- Will?
- I'm fine.

36
00:03:45,743 --> 00:03:47,828
And Rasmussen changed his vote.

37
00:03:47,829 --> 00:03:49,955
Well, he's been waiting a
long time to pay me back.

38
00:03:49,956 --> 00:03:52,583
Creating a deadlock in Rhode
Island just gives him the spotlight.

39
00:03:52,584 --> 00:03:53,636
He can be convinced.

40
00:03:53,637 --> 00:03:55,043
Meeting's set for this afternoon.

41
00:03:55,044 --> 00:03:57,337
Yes, but if we can't,
we lose Rhode Island.

42
00:03:57,338 --> 00:03:59,840
He seems to be acting
alone, but that could change.

43
00:03:59,841 --> 00:04:01,549
We will make gains this weekend.

44
00:04:01,550 --> 00:04:04,112
- Shore things up by Monday's vote.
- Mmm.

45
00:04:06,681 --> 00:04:10,684
Mr. President, it's possible we may
need to consider the other option.

46
00:04:10,685 --> 00:04:12,645
A rescheduled election.

47
00:04:12,646 --> 00:04:14,709
No, no, I'll get Rasmussen back in line,

48
00:04:14,710 --> 00:04:17,247
and turn some others along the way.

49
00:04:17,248 --> 00:04:19,900
We've arranged sit-downs
for you and Madam President

50
00:04:19,901 --> 00:04:21,528
with all of the small states.

51
00:04:21,529 --> 00:04:24,615
LeAnn and I can go door-to-door
in Rayburn, Longworth and Cannon.

52
00:04:24,616 --> 00:04:27,301
Three of my favorite
buildings after this one.

53
00:04:28,202 --> 00:04:29,553
What is it?

54
00:04:29,996 --> 00:04:34,625
The McMurdo Research
Facility in Antarctica.

55
00:04:34,626 --> 00:04:38,128
All live feed communications
have ceased or been disconnected.

56
00:04:38,129 --> 00:04:40,714
However, we have been
receiving texts and e-mails

57
00:04:40,715 --> 00:04:43,425
from an American
scientist stationed there,

58
00:04:43,426 --> 00:04:48,430
claiming that an unidentified
occupying military force has landed.

59
00:04:48,431 --> 00:04:50,808
You think the soldiers are Russian?

60
00:04:50,809 --> 00:04:52,184
He thinks so.

61
00:04:53,477 --> 00:04:56,104
They arrived via
unmarked research vessels.

62
00:04:56,105 --> 00:04:59,316
We reached out to Moscow, but they're
denying any official involvement.

63
00:04:59,317 --> 00:05:00,859
Surprise, surprise.

64
00:05:00,860 --> 00:05:03,696
It's a brazen act of aggression.

65
00:05:03,697 --> 00:05:04,780
Well, it's a crime of opportunity.

66
00:05:04,781 --> 00:05:08,033
Petrov thinks the US government
has their eye off the ball.

67
00:05:08,034 --> 00:05:09,743
Well, it doesn't.

68
00:05:09,744 --> 00:05:12,830
Cathy, what's the most recent
communication we've received?

69
00:05:12,831 --> 00:05:16,624
What I'm saying is this aggression
in Antarctica, by powers unknown,

70
00:05:16,625 --> 00:05:18,433
is a direct result of the uncertainty

71
00:05:18,434 --> 00:05:20,337
that our presidential
situation has wrought.

72
00:05:20,338 --> 00:05:22,965
Congressman Watts, since
when is Antarctica a concern

73
00:05:22,966 --> 00:05:25,398
for the Congressional Black Caucus?

74
00:05:25,426 --> 00:05:27,010
I hope you're not
implying that the caucus

75
00:05:27,011 --> 00:05:29,429
shouldn't have a voice
in international affairs.

76
00:05:29,430 --> 00:05:33,242
I hope that's not what I'm
hearing. Thank you, Randy.

77
00:05:33,893 --> 00:05:36,228
I've called a meeting tomorrow,

78
00:05:36,229 --> 00:05:38,563
for the Congressional Black
Caucus, to discuss our positions,

79
00:05:38,564 --> 00:05:42,317
both foreign and domestic,
before Monday's presidential vote.

80
00:05:42,318 --> 00:05:44,486
Are you considering shifting
your support to Conway?

81
00:05:44,487 --> 00:05:46,440
Well, all I can say at this time,

82
00:05:46,441 --> 00:05:48,991
that the most important
thing is unity...

83
00:05:48,992 --> 00:05:52,452
and the public's faith in
the institution of democracy.

84
00:05:52,453 --> 00:05:54,371
That's all for now.

85
00:05:57,834 --> 00:05:59,106
You are in violation

86
00:05:59,107 --> 00:06:00,710
of the Antarctic Treaty System.

87
00:06:00,711 --> 00:06:04,022
A treaty the USSR signed in 1959.

88
00:06:04,047 --> 00:06:05,320
We're a new country.

89
00:06:05,345 --> 00:06:07,300
So you're admitting
they are your troops.

90
00:06:07,301 --> 00:06:10,270
I'm simply stating I need
to interpret agreements

91
00:06:10,271 --> 00:06:11,805
signed by the Soviet Union.

92
00:06:11,806 --> 00:06:15,392
In which case, you'd be throwing
out decades of disarmament progress.

93
00:06:15,393 --> 00:06:19,562
The Cold War is over,
Mrs. Acting President.

94
00:06:19,563 --> 00:06:22,232
Well, then maybe we need
to revisit the treaty.

95
00:06:22,233 --> 00:06:24,399
I'm not interested in
revisiting a treaty.

96
00:06:24,400 --> 00:06:27,092
I'm interested in staying in power.

97
00:06:27,749 --> 00:06:29,281
And, Claire...

98
00:06:29,282 --> 00:06:32,242
if I could be so familiar.

99
00:06:32,243 --> 00:06:34,443
In my country...

100
00:06:34,788 --> 00:06:37,640
once you're in office, you don't let go.

101
00:06:38,666 --> 00:06:41,352
- Viktor.
- Mr. President.

102
00:06:41,752 --> 00:06:44,587
You Americans certainly have
a lot of presidents these days.

103
00:06:44,588 --> 00:06:46,518
Yes, you may think
that we're distracted,

104
00:06:46,519 --> 00:06:48,383
but don't fool yourself.

105
00:06:48,384 --> 00:06:53,555
Distracted? No. No, no. Divided, yes.

106
00:06:53,556 --> 00:06:56,474
American democracy is
stronger than your sad attempt

107
00:06:56,475 --> 00:06:58,894
to take advantage of a bump in the road.

108
00:06:58,895 --> 00:07:02,355
Oh, Francis, my eyes are on a landscape

109
00:07:02,356 --> 00:07:06,502
that is so much bigger
than any one of your roads.

110
00:07:11,657 --> 00:07:13,110
Well, Congressman Rasmussen,

111
00:07:13,111 --> 00:07:14,785
we're just surprised to see you come out

112
00:07:14,786 --> 00:07:16,995
on the wrong end of this, is all.

113
00:07:16,996 --> 00:07:19,157
I mean, I suppose it must've been hard

114
00:07:19,158 --> 00:07:21,083
losing the House majority leadership

115
00:07:21,084 --> 00:07:22,709
all those years ago,

116
00:07:22,710 --> 00:07:28,048
but there is a difference between
being bitter and being treasonous.

117
00:07:28,049 --> 00:07:30,112
If it's new committee
assignments you'd like,

118
00:07:30,113 --> 00:07:31,844
we can certainly arrange that.

119
00:07:31,845 --> 00:07:33,487
It's over.

120
00:07:34,254 --> 00:07:38,641
Markets are in free fall. Consumer
confidence is at an all-time low.

121
00:07:38,642 --> 00:07:42,855
What is this? A doodad? A
doohickey? A... pointless award?

122
00:07:42,856 --> 00:07:45,565
Did I mention I hate useless things?

123
00:07:45,566 --> 00:07:48,151
This nation is coming
apart at the seams.

124
00:07:48,152 --> 00:07:51,196
Because of you, because of what
you've done to this country.

125
00:07:51,197 --> 00:07:53,348
There it is. How the righteous love

126
00:07:53,349 --> 00:07:55,325
to bemoan their role
in middle management,

127
00:07:55,326 --> 00:07:59,282
and condemn those who actually
might have an effect on the world.

128
00:07:59,956 --> 00:08:03,375
- Think of the party, Congressman.
- I am.

129
00:08:03,376 --> 00:08:05,961
What did you think would happen
when you came in here, Francis?

130
00:08:05,962 --> 00:08:07,629
That you could threaten me,

131
00:08:07,630 --> 00:08:11,133
tell me the DNC'll throw money
at my primary opponent next cycle

132
00:08:11,134 --> 00:08:14,677
if I don't play ball? Or
were you gonna flatter me?

133
00:08:14,678 --> 00:08:18,891
"Elder of the party" this,
"bellwether senior member" that.

134
00:08:18,892 --> 00:08:20,561
You know, I'm somewhat delighted

135
00:08:20,562 --> 00:08:23,603
that you developed a
personality at this late date.

136
00:08:23,604 --> 00:08:25,772
But I would ask you to rethink this.

137
00:08:25,773 --> 00:08:29,651
To come out this way on this thing
in the twilight of your career

138
00:08:29,652 --> 00:08:32,612
will do nothing but
sully your Wikipedia page.

139
00:08:32,613 --> 00:08:34,965
You have no idea, do you?

140
00:08:35,533 --> 00:08:41,222
No one hears you walking the halls
of this building... not anymore.

141
00:08:44,500 --> 00:08:47,311
- Who are you working for?
- Excuse me?

142
00:08:47,753 --> 00:08:51,839
I served next to your
jellied spine for two decades.

143
00:08:51,840 --> 00:08:54,386
You've never shown an ounce of courage.

144
00:08:54,387 --> 00:08:57,012
You don't have what
it takes for revenge.

145
00:08:57,013 --> 00:09:00,658
So someone must have offered
you something rather sweet.

146
00:09:02,226 --> 00:09:05,371
Take all of the two hours
you need to reconsider.

147
00:09:13,737 --> 00:09:16,674
One more thing for your signature.

148
00:09:30,754 --> 00:09:33,441
- You can leave this with me.
- Of course.

149
00:09:35,634 --> 00:09:39,112
- And Miss Harvey's waiting.
- Oh, please send her in.

150
00:09:45,186 --> 00:09:47,020
I'm on my way back to Rayburn.

151
00:09:47,021 --> 00:09:49,039
You close the door, please?

152
00:09:53,902 --> 00:09:56,154
What are our chances with a revote?

153
00:09:56,155 --> 00:09:58,632
Or, rather, what are
the president's chances?

154
00:10:02,703 --> 00:10:04,096
I want to know.

155
00:10:04,663 --> 00:10:08,309
If it was in all 50 states, not good.

156
00:10:08,876 --> 00:10:12,521
His unfavorables are still
high. Yours are still not.

157
00:10:13,089 --> 00:10:15,632
But it's hard to argue with
close to 30 years' experience.

158
00:10:15,633 --> 00:10:16,930
Maybe the president is right.

159
00:10:16,931 --> 00:10:18,926
Maybe he does have a
better chance in the House.

160
00:10:18,927 --> 00:10:21,490
Don't defer, LeAnn.
Tell me what you think.

161
00:10:22,223 --> 00:10:25,808
The revote in Tennessee
and Ohio is his best shot.

162
00:10:25,809 --> 00:10:31,332
While it's what's best for Frank,
it may not be what's best for you.

163
00:10:32,608 --> 00:10:34,585
Thank you.

164
00:10:43,369 --> 00:10:44,911
You seem like a reasonable man.

165
00:10:44,912 --> 00:10:47,955
I know how to listen,
if that's what you mean.

166
00:10:47,956 --> 00:10:49,166
If the president regains his office...

167
00:10:49,167 --> 00:10:52,544
"If" doesn't make for
a winning opener, Alex.

168
00:10:52,545 --> 00:10:54,796
I'm here because I think he can win.

169
00:10:54,797 --> 00:10:57,215
We often don't see eye to eye, he and I.

170
00:10:57,216 --> 00:11:00,611
Uh, and yet, there are
ways we can work together.

171
00:11:02,179 --> 00:11:05,348
Okay, maybe I made a
mistake with the committee.

172
00:11:05,349 --> 00:11:06,683
I can be self-righteous.

173
00:11:06,684 --> 00:11:08,726
I'm trying to clean up
the party. Our party.

174
00:11:08,727 --> 00:11:10,645
And I end up speaking out of turn.

175
00:11:10,646 --> 00:11:15,475
It's enough with the, "It's me, not
you." What can you deliver, Alex?

176
00:11:15,476 --> 00:11:16,990
I can get out of the way.

177
00:11:16,991 --> 00:11:20,188
Well, that sounds vague and inadequate.

178
00:11:22,200 --> 00:11:25,010
I want to know what's up with
Terry Womack and the Black Caucus.

179
00:11:25,911 --> 00:11:28,663
The president and Congressman
Womack, they have a good relationship.

180
00:11:28,664 --> 00:11:32,851
Oh, I'm sure they do. But I would
hate to see Terry let you down.

181
00:11:34,920 --> 00:11:38,816
If we lose Womack and the Black
Caucus, we're done on Monday.

182
00:11:39,383 --> 00:11:41,676
There are a few freshmen
I could reach out to.

183
00:11:41,677 --> 00:11:43,946
They might help keep the CBC together.

184
00:11:45,639 --> 00:11:47,349
You wanna be minority whip.

185
00:11:47,350 --> 00:11:49,702
Only if I deserve to be.

186
00:11:50,353 --> 00:11:51,519
Huh.

187
00:11:51,520 --> 00:11:55,732
I'm trying to get back. Congressman
Lewis is 20 minutes late as usual.

188
00:11:55,733 --> 00:11:58,586
Is there a spot for him
on financial services?

189
00:12:00,613 --> 00:12:02,172
Uh-huh.

190
00:12:08,579 --> 00:12:12,015
Doug, stay where you are.

191
00:12:12,666 --> 00:12:15,460
I thought you said you were
telling the CIA to stand down.

192
00:12:15,461 --> 00:12:18,921
I did. So, contrary to your
assurances that Macallan would behave,

193
00:12:18,922 --> 00:12:20,173
we're now dealing with a leak.

194
00:12:20,174 --> 00:12:23,318
- He doesn't want trouble.
- How do you know what he wants?

195
00:12:24,137 --> 00:12:26,012
You find him and you get him here.

196
00:12:26,013 --> 00:12:29,766
As soon as he knows we've backed
off, he will disappear. I promise.

197
00:12:29,767 --> 00:12:32,578
No, that word means nothing to me.

198
00:12:41,237 --> 00:12:42,404
You have a statement?

199
00:12:42,405 --> 00:12:45,128
You anticipate any other
big WikiLeaks info dumps

200
00:12:45,129 --> 00:12:46,283
coming down the pike?

201
00:12:46,284 --> 00:12:49,369
- You'll know...
- When I need to know. I know.

202
00:12:49,370 --> 00:12:52,289
Which is apparently
whenever it hits CNN.

203
00:12:52,290 --> 00:12:55,375
FBI was on high alert. They
investigated every threat.

204
00:12:55,376 --> 00:12:57,169
Calm down.

205
00:12:57,170 --> 00:13:01,130
I am calm, Doug. The public's not calm.

206
00:13:01,131 --> 00:13:04,384
Every misstep is
highlighted and heightened.

207
00:13:04,385 --> 00:13:07,094
They wanna know what
happened with their election.

208
00:13:07,095 --> 00:13:08,180
We're fixing it.

209
00:13:08,181 --> 00:13:11,575
Yeah... that's what they're afraid of.

210
00:13:12,226 --> 00:13:15,829
The statement's about the
FBI. Everybody trusts the FBI.

211
00:13:21,485 --> 00:13:23,796
Hey, who's Lisa Williams?

212
00:13:25,823 --> 00:13:28,616
I don't know. Why?

213
00:13:28,617 --> 00:13:30,651
Oh, somebody from the Herald called.

214
00:13:30,652 --> 00:13:32,954
They were just wondering
if you knew her.

215
00:13:34,915 --> 00:13:36,516
I don't.

216
00:13:43,882 --> 00:13:46,234
I need five minutes with this.

217
00:13:50,806 --> 00:13:52,825
- Open or closed?
- Closed.

218
00:14:10,326 --> 00:14:11,909
Am I good with that statement?

219
00:14:11,910 --> 00:14:14,304
I marked it up. It's on my desk.

220
00:15:00,167 --> 00:15:02,199
Throwing the election
back to the states,

221
00:15:02,200 --> 00:15:03,628
it gives us less certainty.

222
00:15:03,629 --> 00:15:06,130
I don't think we can
get it done by Monday.

223
00:15:06,131 --> 00:15:07,799
We've been working at
it for weeks, Francis.

224
00:15:07,800 --> 00:15:09,426
Claire, don't let the
office make you careful.

225
00:15:09,427 --> 00:15:10,968
The halls of Congress
have never failed me.

226
00:15:10,969 --> 00:15:14,847
Yes, but the career politicians,
they are more entrenched than ever.

227
00:15:14,848 --> 00:15:17,892
There's a new House, with
members you don't know.

228
00:15:17,893 --> 00:15:19,403
I think we need to
move the playing field

229
00:15:19,404 --> 00:15:21,020
back to the imaginations of the people.

230
00:15:21,021 --> 00:15:24,148
Oh, you have that much faith in
the voters in Tennessee and Ohio?

231
00:15:24,149 --> 00:15:27,527
They're going to choose
you. I just know it.

232
00:15:27,528 --> 00:15:30,363
All right.

233
00:15:30,364 --> 00:15:34,075
Set up a meeting. I'll
hear them all out. You win.

234
00:15:34,076 --> 00:15:36,828
Both of us. Both of us.

235
00:15:37,996 --> 00:15:39,163
Good evening.

236
00:15:39,164 --> 00:15:40,724
Eric.

237
00:15:41,875 --> 00:15:43,668
- Feel like you're outside?
- Mmm.

238
00:15:43,669 --> 00:15:45,545
Aren't you supposed to
limit it to 30 minutes?

239
00:15:45,546 --> 00:15:48,214
- Oh, God, Jesus, no.
- Not if you calibrate the workout.

240
00:15:48,215 --> 00:15:49,757
Oh, I'm feeling fine.

241
00:15:49,758 --> 00:15:51,637
It's all about listening to your body.

242
00:15:51,638 --> 00:15:53,594
It'll tell you when it's time to rest.

243
00:15:54,430 --> 00:15:57,014
You should listen to the doctors
some of the time, Francis.

244
00:16:05,232 --> 00:16:09,336
I know the Underwoods were worried.
Because they were ready to deal.

245
00:16:09,903 --> 00:16:11,504
To deal with me.

246
00:16:11,739 --> 00:16:14,883
- You're sure it's for real?
- Sure as I can be.

247
00:16:16,201 --> 00:16:18,511
Okay, we'll be in touch.

248
00:16:18,829 --> 00:16:20,180
Okay.

249
00:16:24,335 --> 00:16:26,103
So call him back.

250
00:16:27,170 --> 00:16:29,464
Making progress on a couple of Dems.

251
00:16:29,465 --> 00:16:31,633
Think Congressman
Alspaugh is about to turn.

252
00:16:31,634 --> 00:16:33,526
We'll see him on Sunday.

253
00:16:35,388 --> 00:16:37,155
Why wait?

254
00:16:38,807 --> 00:16:42,519
We need to meet with the
Congressional Black Caucus. Tomorrow.

255
00:16:42,520 --> 00:16:44,788
They're in Underwood's pocket.

256
00:16:45,648 --> 00:16:48,775
Yeah. Can you give us
a second, everybody?

257
00:16:48,776 --> 00:16:50,026
Uh, sure.

258
00:16:50,027 --> 00:16:51,754
Come on.

259
00:16:53,113 --> 00:16:55,090
Thank you.

260
00:17:02,039 --> 00:17:04,180
I just got word from somebody inside.

261
00:17:04,181 --> 00:17:07,168
Womack is losing control of the caucus.

262
00:17:08,796 --> 00:17:10,939
If you win them, you win Monday.

263
00:17:11,757 --> 00:17:13,550
We should try.

264
00:17:13,551 --> 00:17:15,610
All right, fine. Let's do it.

265
00:17:17,304 --> 00:17:19,472
- I'll set it up.
- Are we done here tonight?

266
00:17:19,473 --> 00:17:21,491
You still have a few calls.

267
00:17:22,100 --> 00:17:24,977
Mark, I was thinking,
maybe we could, um...

268
00:17:24,978 --> 00:17:28,105
cancel tomorrow night in Philadelphia.

269
00:17:28,106 --> 00:17:32,127
We can't cancel any trip,
or any event. We shouldn't.

270
00:17:32,778 --> 00:17:35,613
A second to catch our breath
would be really helpful, I think.

271
00:17:35,614 --> 00:17:39,366
This is what it is, Hannah. If
you need to take a break, you can.

272
00:17:39,367 --> 00:17:41,970
Darling, we haven't slept all week.

273
00:17:42,955 --> 00:17:44,514
You're exhausted.

274
00:17:52,047 --> 00:17:55,733
- I... just an idea.
- What's my next call?

275
00:18:08,856 --> 00:18:11,041
But you're not enjoying yourself.

276
00:18:12,067 --> 00:18:14,276
Because he won't listen to me.

277
00:18:14,277 --> 00:18:16,237
He can't win in the House.

278
00:18:16,238 --> 00:18:19,842
I'm not talking about
him. I'm talking about you.

279
00:18:22,369 --> 00:18:24,996
What difference does it make
whether I'm enjoying myself or not?

280
00:18:24,997 --> 00:18:27,140
Isn't that what this is all about?

281
00:18:28,501 --> 00:18:32,879
Can't you just... live
in it for a second?

282
00:18:32,880 --> 00:18:34,714
You are in charge.

283
00:18:34,715 --> 00:18:37,067
- Yes, but...
- Come here.

284
00:18:38,385 --> 00:18:40,570
Come.

285
00:18:45,809 --> 00:18:48,120
You could wake up tomorrow...

286
00:18:48,854 --> 00:18:50,372
and...

287
00:18:51,231 --> 00:18:54,901
I don't know, feed the children...

288
00:18:54,902 --> 00:18:56,360
... start a war...

289
00:18:56,361 --> 00:18:58,463
You know what? I'm sorry.

290
00:18:59,197 --> 00:19:01,967
I shouldn't think out loud, that's all.

291
00:19:04,077 --> 00:19:05,369
No, you're right.

292
00:19:05,370 --> 00:19:09,266
Let's not talk about anything
that happens outside of this room.

293
00:21:23,717 --> 00:21:27,136
The sanctions are meant to target
the oligarch class in Russia.

294
00:21:27,137 --> 00:21:28,903
And the more members we have on board

295
00:21:28,904 --> 00:21:30,807
before I sit down with President Petrov,

296
00:21:30,808 --> 00:21:33,243
the quicker this will resolve itself.

297
00:21:34,561 --> 00:21:38,272
We are. We are prepared to release
some of our strategic reserves

298
00:21:38,273 --> 00:21:39,565
to keep prices down.

299
00:21:39,566 --> 00:21:44,587
Contact Ambassador Caspi
and buy me 15 more minutes.

300
00:21:45,155 --> 00:21:49,134
Uh, please don't think of this
as a deal with the Underwoods.

301
00:21:49,785 --> 00:21:52,720
To be frank, they
could be gone by Monday.

302
00:21:53,496 --> 00:21:55,748
That's not what I wish, of course.

303
00:21:55,749 --> 00:22:00,812
But know that you are working more
with me on this than with them.

304
00:22:22,192 --> 00:22:23,693
Congressman Watts.

305
00:22:23,694 --> 00:22:26,070
Governor, we're so happy
you could join us today.

306
00:22:26,071 --> 00:22:29,031
As am I. The black community
deserves so much better

307
00:22:29,032 --> 00:22:30,783
than Frank Underwood's failed policies.

308
00:22:30,784 --> 00:22:33,619
That we can agree on. I look forward
to hearing about your platform.

309
00:22:33,620 --> 00:22:34,703
- Shall we?
- Oh, no, I'm sorry.

310
00:22:34,704 --> 00:22:38,350
We have just a bit more private
caucus business to attend to first.

311
00:22:38,917 --> 00:22:40,376
Excuse me?

312
00:22:40,377 --> 00:22:42,336
This is a meeting of the
Congressional Black Caucus.

313
00:22:42,337 --> 00:22:45,256
Well, I understand.
That's... why we're here.

314
00:22:45,257 --> 00:22:48,717
I was told 9:00, and
it's already been an hour.

315
00:22:48,718 --> 00:22:49,844
You should have been told 10:00.

316
00:22:49,845 --> 00:22:53,323
And you will get your
opportunity to address us.

317
00:22:54,266 --> 00:22:57,076
I appreciate your patience.

318
00:23:05,443 --> 00:23:07,236
I'm not gonna beg.

319
00:23:07,237 --> 00:23:09,756
No, you're gonna smile.

320
00:23:13,910 --> 00:23:16,078
The Muhammed Kalabi they
arrested in Tennessee

321
00:23:16,079 --> 00:23:17,872
is not the Kalabi connected to ICO.

322
00:23:17,873 --> 00:23:20,207
Can you prove it wasn't
just a bureaucratic mix-up?

323
00:23:20,208 --> 00:23:21,476
Uh, not yet.

324
00:23:24,797 --> 00:23:26,005
What else?

325
00:23:26,006 --> 00:23:29,550
Uh, final voter suppression lawsuit
was kicked back to the lower court,

326
00:23:29,551 --> 00:23:33,070
- SCOTUS deadlock is feeding the chaos.
- Do you read the paper, Sean?

327
00:23:33,388 --> 00:23:36,073
Billy got a byline on
that three days ago.

328
00:23:37,725 --> 00:23:38,851
Declaration of war?

329
00:23:38,852 --> 00:23:41,520
Underwood was so evangelical,
but hasn't mentioned it in weeks.

330
00:23:41,521 --> 00:23:44,624
All right, that's enough. That's enough.

331
00:23:49,696 --> 00:23:51,214
Lisa Williams.

332
00:23:53,200 --> 00:23:54,926
What about Lisa Williams?

333
00:23:55,577 --> 00:23:56,785
I saw you talking.

334
00:23:56,786 --> 00:23:59,663
I thought she might be
a lead worth pursuing.

335
00:23:59,664 --> 00:24:00,747
She's not.

336
00:24:00,748 --> 00:24:02,655
She's using what she read in the paper

337
00:24:02,656 --> 00:24:04,293
to get attention or a little cash.

338
00:24:04,294 --> 00:24:06,584
I did some digging.
She and Rachel Posner...

339
00:24:06,585 --> 00:24:09,131
both went to a church
called the Fellowship.

340
00:24:09,132 --> 00:24:11,008
- Rachel disappeared...
- Stop.

341
00:24:11,009 --> 00:24:13,135
No more conspiracy
theories about dead girls.

342
00:24:13,136 --> 00:24:14,595
But people love stories
about dead girls.

343
00:24:14,596 --> 00:24:19,201
The next House vote is
the day after tomorrow.

344
00:24:19,476 --> 00:24:24,813
The natives are restless, outraged,
just plain scared, actually.

345
00:24:24,814 --> 00:24:28,960
They want to wake up in the
America that they fell asleep in.

346
00:24:35,075 --> 00:24:40,263
Dig deeper on the Kalabi lead. See
if you can connect Underwood to it.

347
00:24:52,175 --> 00:24:56,321
So, this is the James S.
Brady press briefing room.

348
00:24:57,764 --> 00:25:01,308
And under our feet is
actually a swimming pool.

349
00:25:01,309 --> 00:25:03,227
It was built by the March of Dimes

350
00:25:03,228 --> 00:25:05,312
for President Franklin
Roosevelt, who had polio.

351
00:25:05,313 --> 00:25:08,732
And this pool was really the
only way for him to exercise.

352
00:25:08,733 --> 00:25:10,317
It was, uh, an open secret

353
00:25:10,318 --> 00:25:12,962
that the president used a
wheelchair most of the time.

354
00:25:13,613 --> 00:25:16,291
Which is why the White
House was one of the first

355
00:25:16,292 --> 00:25:19,160
wheelchair-accessible
government buildings.

356
00:25:19,161 --> 00:25:22,096
- But everyone pretended he could walk?
- Yes.

357
00:25:22,664 --> 00:25:25,057
- That's a bit of grace.
- Yeah.

358
00:25:25,292 --> 00:25:26,750
A thing not spoken?

359
00:25:26,751 --> 00:25:30,337
Yeah, this building literally
changed because of his disability.

360
00:25:30,338 --> 00:25:33,882
But people just respected
him, respected his, uh...

361
00:25:33,883 --> 00:25:35,092
Right.

362
00:25:35,093 --> 00:25:37,781
That office used to
carry so much weight,

363
00:25:37,782 --> 00:25:39,555
so much, uh, respect.

364
00:25:39,556 --> 00:25:41,515
You don't think it does anymore?

365
00:25:41,516 --> 00:25:45,436
No, oh, no, no! I'm not saying that.

366
00:25:45,437 --> 00:25:48,873
I was giving you a hard
time. I know what you meant.

367
00:25:51,526 --> 00:25:55,004
Anyway, sorry, didn't
mean to hijack the tour.

368
00:25:57,532 --> 00:26:00,259
Moving on...

369
00:26:07,917 --> 00:26:10,895
Gentlemen, we're ready for you now.

370
00:26:16,801 --> 00:26:19,395
I could come here
today and talk about...

371
00:26:19,396 --> 00:26:22,098
federal sentencing disparities
between crack and cocaine,

372
00:26:22,099 --> 00:26:24,124
which obviously are racially motivated.

373
00:26:24,125 --> 00:26:26,143
I'm sorry, but are you asserting

374
00:26:26,144 --> 00:26:28,980
that the crack epidemic is
exclusive to the black community?

375
00:26:28,981 --> 00:26:30,814
No, not at all.

376
00:26:30,815 --> 00:26:34,485
But I think it's fair to say
that sentencing discrepancies

377
00:26:34,486 --> 00:26:37,863
have disproportionately
affected minority populations.

378
00:26:37,864 --> 00:26:39,865
We're not one-issue voters.

379
00:26:39,866 --> 00:26:41,700
Of course not. That's
what I'm getting at.

380
00:26:41,701 --> 00:26:44,161
The whole point of today's
meeting is to broaden our platform.

381
00:26:44,162 --> 00:26:46,497
Republicans are always
dwelling on crime, Governor.

382
00:26:46,498 --> 00:26:48,207
Well, I'm here to have a dialogue.

383
00:26:48,208 --> 00:26:50,459
We expected you to come
in here with a plan.

384
00:26:50,460 --> 00:26:51,960
And I do have a plan.

385
00:26:51,961 --> 00:26:55,464
Concentrating on a few pieces of
onerous legislation is not a plan.

386
00:26:55,465 --> 00:26:57,383
- Let's be clear...
- That kind of talk we've heard before.

387
00:26:57,384 --> 00:26:59,218
- I'm a Republican.
- Oh, here we go.

388
00:26:59,219 --> 00:27:00,636
You're not listening.

389
00:27:00,637 --> 00:27:04,098
Look, I want to help
you help yourselves.

390
00:27:09,687 --> 00:27:11,147
Unbelievable.

391
00:27:11,148 --> 00:27:13,482
That's my platform.

392
00:27:13,483 --> 00:27:17,128
Well... I've heard enough.

393
00:27:19,906 --> 00:27:24,035
So, does anyone else have any
questions for the governor?

394
00:27:24,036 --> 00:27:26,345
Well, I have a question.

395
00:27:27,664 --> 00:27:30,475
What has Francis Underwood
ever done for you?

396
00:27:32,585 --> 00:27:37,399
This little charade this morning...

397
00:27:37,757 --> 00:27:40,676
- Well done. Quite an act.
- Will.

398
00:27:40,677 --> 00:27:42,178
- Governor.
- No, but really.

399
00:27:42,179 --> 00:27:44,864
See, you may have differences with me...

400
00:27:45,848 --> 00:27:48,809
You may have genuine
differences with me.

401
00:27:48,810 --> 00:27:51,829
But see, that's the
operative word, "genuine."

402
00:27:52,564 --> 00:27:54,398
Can someone here please explain to me

403
00:27:54,399 --> 00:27:57,919
why you all are still so loyal to
Francis Underwood and the Democrats?

404
00:28:01,864 --> 00:28:03,407
One of these days

405
00:28:03,408 --> 00:28:05,669
the members of this room will realize

406
00:28:05,670 --> 00:28:08,995
how much you actually
prop up the status quo.

407
00:28:10,457 --> 00:28:11,957
And how's that working for ya?

408
00:28:11,958 --> 00:28:15,895
Will, why don't we thank the
gentlemen and women for their time?

409
00:28:32,061 --> 00:28:33,913
You've made a mistake.

410
00:28:37,775 --> 00:28:41,445
What we're proposing is a
new election in the two states

411
00:28:41,446 --> 00:28:45,407
that weren't able to provide valid
electors by the safe harbor date.

412
00:28:45,408 --> 00:28:46,992
Can we even do this?

413
00:28:46,993 --> 00:28:49,303
Hold on. Hold on, everyone!

414
00:28:49,829 --> 00:28:53,082
I'd like to know, where does the
attorney general stand on this?

415
00:28:53,083 --> 00:28:55,126
Looking into it, but on board.

416
00:28:55,127 --> 00:28:59,213
Bob, there's no precedent
here. There's no playbook.

417
00:28:59,214 --> 00:29:01,667
We can look to the
Constitution for inspiration,

418
00:29:01,668 --> 00:29:03,634
but not for instruction.

419
00:29:03,635 --> 00:29:06,262
Thursday's vote showed Democrats
are coming to their senses.

420
00:29:06,263 --> 00:29:08,054
We lost one vote, Jordan.

421
00:29:08,055 --> 00:29:10,266
We can go back and
forth like this forever.

422
00:29:10,267 --> 00:29:13,435
As one of the few Republicans
in the room, it needs to be said.

423
00:29:13,436 --> 00:29:17,148
The stability of our country is
more important than the presidency.

424
00:29:17,149 --> 00:29:18,641
Not if people believe the president

425
00:29:18,642 --> 00:29:20,442
is being chosen behind closed doors.

426
00:29:20,443 --> 00:29:23,779
A process that is laid
out in the Constitution.

427
00:29:23,780 --> 00:29:26,990
The same process that can keep us
gridlocked for the next two years.

428
00:29:26,991 --> 00:29:29,660
The House should and will decide.

429
00:29:29,661 --> 00:29:32,096
Delaware has one congressman.

430
00:29:32,330 --> 00:29:35,624
How will that play in Austin
when your opponent in two years

431
00:29:35,625 --> 00:29:38,001
tells voters that you
allowed a state like Delaware

432
00:29:38,002 --> 00:29:40,104
to have the same influence as Texas?

433
00:29:40,588 --> 00:29:45,067
A lot of you incumbents will be
in danger of losing your seats.

434
00:29:48,930 --> 00:29:50,681
And there it is.

435
00:29:50,682 --> 00:29:53,284
Look. Look at them.

436
00:29:53,851 --> 00:29:57,413
That is the look of contemplating loss.

437
00:29:58,606 --> 00:30:00,207
Loss...

438
00:30:01,526 --> 00:30:06,112
the only constituent that anyone
in this room really listens to.

439
00:30:06,113 --> 00:30:09,032
I, for one, would like to hear how
the president feels about all this.

440
00:30:09,033 --> 00:30:10,927
- Well, I've made my...
- I think we could...

441
00:30:11,453 --> 00:30:14,705
No, Mr. President. You
haven't spoken much here.

442
00:30:14,706 --> 00:30:17,958
We believe in one person, one vote.

443
00:30:17,959 --> 00:30:21,187
If we take that away,
then what is this country?

444
00:30:22,505 --> 00:30:26,526
- Isn't that right, Mr. President?
- Absolutely, Madam President.

445
00:30:32,890 --> 00:30:35,559
Oh, wait, hold it. Let
me just move down a bit.

446
00:30:35,560 --> 00:30:37,769
I keep slipping off the podium.

447
00:30:37,770 --> 00:30:39,188
- Oh, you got me.
- That better?

448
00:30:39,189 --> 00:30:42,959
Yeah, yeah, yeah...

449
00:30:45,320 --> 00:30:47,654
So the Lincoln...

450
00:30:47,655 --> 00:30:50,532
- That was actually his office?
- Can we... can we not?

451
00:30:50,533 --> 00:30:52,743
- Can you... can you move me up a bit?
- Oh, yeah.

452
00:30:52,744 --> 00:30:55,496
Okay.

453
00:31:07,550 --> 00:31:10,344
- How'd it go?
- We'll see.

454
00:31:10,345 --> 00:31:13,179
I need you to look over the
Antarctica statement again.

455
00:31:13,180 --> 00:31:14,640
Whatever you need.

456
00:31:14,641 --> 00:31:16,909
- Walk with me?
- Mmm.

457
00:31:18,561 --> 00:31:20,537
I'm worried about Francis.

458
00:31:21,105 --> 00:31:24,667
- Why?
- I might need you to save me.

459
00:31:30,448 --> 00:31:32,574
- This is not going to work.
- Why?

460
00:31:32,575 --> 00:31:34,743
If he loses, I still lose.

461
00:31:34,744 --> 00:31:37,138
He cannot be on the outside looking in.

462
00:31:37,705 --> 00:31:38,997
He'll win.

463
00:31:38,998 --> 00:31:43,435
- You actually believe that?
- Sure... why not?

464
00:31:47,674 --> 00:31:51,051
It was a wager. The potential
gains made it necessary.

465
00:31:51,052 --> 00:31:53,929
It was a complete waste of my time.

466
00:31:53,930 --> 00:31:55,722
If you don't start
turning things around,

467
00:31:55,723 --> 00:31:58,701
you're going to be out on your
fucking ass. You understand?

468
00:32:00,102 --> 00:32:01,453
I do.

469
00:32:06,484 --> 00:32:08,753
I should explain
something to you, Governor.

470
00:32:11,656 --> 00:32:14,676
If you ever talk to
me like that again...

471
00:32:15,242 --> 00:32:18,638
I'll make sure you never win
another election in your life.

472
00:32:20,707 --> 00:32:23,935
- Are you threatening me?
- No, I'm enlightening you.

473
00:33:32,362 --> 00:33:33,675
Hey.

474
00:33:33,676 --> 00:33:35,196
Governor Conway. Hello, sir.

475
00:33:35,197 --> 00:33:37,699
How you doin'? Take a peek,

476
00:33:37,700 --> 00:33:40,636
- if you don't mind, Captain?
- Governor.

477
00:33:41,579 --> 00:33:43,455
I used to fly, too, you know.

478
00:33:43,456 --> 00:33:45,541
What kinda max thrust
you get on these engines?

479
00:33:45,542 --> 00:33:48,460
- Uh, about 6,400 pounds.
- 6,400 pounds.

480
00:33:48,461 --> 00:33:51,212
I used to fly something a
lot bigger, a lot more power,

481
00:33:51,213 --> 00:33:54,257
but it was so weighed
down with all the weaponry,

482
00:33:54,258 --> 00:33:57,904
it was like flying a tank.

483
00:33:58,888 --> 00:34:00,013
Hey, um,

484
00:34:00,014 --> 00:34:03,058
why don't you let me in for a
second, just flip me the controls?

485
00:34:03,059 --> 00:34:04,393
I'm sorry, sir. I can't.

486
00:34:04,394 --> 00:34:07,813
Come on, I... I just want to feel
the weight on the stick for a second.

487
00:34:07,814 --> 00:34:09,898
I wish I could, but, uh,
you're not on the flight plan.

488
00:34:09,899 --> 00:34:13,860
You know I've been in situations,
where, like, I've been shot at.

489
00:34:13,861 --> 00:34:14,861
You realize that, right?

490
00:34:14,862 --> 00:34:18,574
You don't think I can handle
a twin engine at 35,000 feet,

491
00:34:18,575 --> 00:34:21,702
straight and level over some farm
country? I mean, come on, sir.

492
00:34:21,703 --> 00:34:22,828
Sir.

493
00:34:22,829 --> 00:34:25,347
You should probably go
back to the main cabin.

494
00:34:29,461 --> 00:34:31,628
You know I'm gonna be
the president, right?

495
00:34:31,629 --> 00:34:34,255
- I hope so, sir.
- You hope so?

496
00:34:34,256 --> 00:34:38,093
- I just mean I'm pulling for you, sir.
- I'm gonna be the president,

497
00:34:38,094 --> 00:34:41,763
and you're going to flip me
those motherfucking controls.

498
00:34:41,764 --> 00:34:43,615
Will, come on.

499
00:34:44,642 --> 00:34:46,643
- What? What do you want?
- I just...

500
00:34:46,644 --> 00:34:49,831
I need to talk to you
for a minute, please.

501
00:34:55,945 --> 00:34:58,279
I was just talking to the pilots.

502
00:34:58,280 --> 00:35:03,385
Okay, I need you to
calm down, okay? Breathe.

503
00:35:04,704 --> 00:35:08,373
- Will, why don't you sit down?
- Why don't you shut the fuck up?

504
00:35:08,374 --> 00:35:11,668
I'll fire you. I'll leave
you on the goddamn tarmac.

505
00:35:13,170 --> 00:35:14,814
Okay.

506
00:35:17,884 --> 00:35:20,652
What the fuck are you gonna do?

507
00:35:23,430 --> 00:35:27,034
Sit down and try and
act like an adult...

508
00:35:27,351 --> 00:35:30,662
or at least like the next
President of the United States.

509
00:35:34,692 --> 00:35:36,043
Sit.

510
00:35:54,921 --> 00:35:59,316
- Who are they?
- Maybe Indonesian secret police?

511
00:36:00,510 --> 00:36:01,986
What if we don't pay?

512
00:36:04,180 --> 00:36:05,948
Are there other buyers?

513
00:36:06,516 --> 00:36:08,075
I don't know.

514
00:36:13,147 --> 00:36:14,773
I'll take care of it.

515
00:36:14,774 --> 00:36:16,567
- How?
- Don't ask how.

516
00:36:16,568 --> 00:36:18,419
I'll get Macallan back.

517
00:36:19,278 --> 00:36:21,630
Obviously, this stays between us.

518
00:36:24,784 --> 00:36:26,718
Say, "Thank you, Doug."

519
00:36:28,120 --> 00:36:30,014
Thank you, Doug.

520
00:36:35,461 --> 00:36:37,337
We met on a bus.

521
00:36:37,338 --> 00:36:38,547
She was listening to music.

522
00:36:38,548 --> 00:36:40,841
And I asked her to go to
this church I used to go to.

523
00:36:40,842 --> 00:36:43,134
- Where was that?
- Maryland.

524
00:36:43,135 --> 00:36:47,598
The Winter's Run Fellowship.
About an hour north of Baltimore.

525
00:36:47,599 --> 00:36:49,200
So, then what?

526
00:36:50,142 --> 00:36:52,102
I don't know. We just...

527
00:36:52,103 --> 00:36:55,331
became friends. And then
it became more than that.

528
00:36:55,898 --> 00:36:57,959
Started spending every night together.

529
00:36:58,610 --> 00:37:00,476
At some point, she
told me about this guy

530
00:37:00,477 --> 00:37:01,987
who used to come around to check on her.

531
00:37:01,988 --> 00:37:04,823
- What'd he look like?
- I never saw him.

532
00:37:04,824 --> 00:37:07,701
Just sometimes she said he was
coming, so she couldn't hang out.

533
00:37:07,702 --> 00:37:09,720
She wouldn't really tell
me anything about him.

534
00:37:12,957 --> 00:37:14,040
Everything okay?

535
00:37:14,041 --> 00:37:16,810
Yeah, do you mind? I just
need to see my friend up there.

536
00:37:24,677 --> 00:37:26,028
Hey.

537
00:37:28,430 --> 00:37:29,949
How are you doing?

538
00:37:31,851 --> 00:37:33,202
Thanks.

539
00:37:33,895 --> 00:37:37,856
You think there's oil there.
You're positioning to stake a claim.

540
00:37:37,857 --> 00:37:40,066
There are no Russian
troops in Antarctica.

541
00:37:42,194 --> 00:37:47,198
We're willing to recommend changes
to the Antarctic Treaty parties.

542
00:37:47,199 --> 00:37:51,286
What happens to you when the
Underwoods are ejected from office?

543
00:37:51,287 --> 00:37:55,373
You teach international
relations at a university?

544
00:37:55,374 --> 00:37:58,043
- Or work for a think tank?
- I haven't thought that far ahead.

545
00:37:58,044 --> 00:37:59,628
I think you have.

546
00:37:59,629 --> 00:38:01,838
How long do you expect
the world to believe

547
00:38:01,839 --> 00:38:03,340
those aren't your soldiers?

548
00:38:03,341 --> 00:38:06,916
Do you like working for the Underwoods?

549
00:38:08,554 --> 00:38:10,447
How loyal are you?

550
00:38:12,308 --> 00:38:17,663
When the time comes, will
you stand with them... or not?

551
00:38:21,358 --> 00:38:23,127
I leave tomorrow.

552
00:38:23,485 --> 00:38:27,488
News of the sanctions will hit Monday.

553
00:38:27,489 --> 00:38:29,341
Let's meet before you go.

554
00:38:33,412 --> 00:38:36,223
You're a career
diplomat, a professional.

555
00:38:37,041 --> 00:38:39,226
Maybe that's why I trust you.

556
00:38:40,336 --> 00:38:42,337
Then you take care of them,

557
00:38:42,338 --> 00:38:45,006
so they feel good and
they take care of you.

558
00:38:45,007 --> 00:38:49,052
So you feel good because... I
mean, Rachel was really beautiful.

559
00:38:49,053 --> 00:38:51,471
You could tell that
from across the street.

560
00:38:51,472 --> 00:38:53,890
I never saw her from
across the street, though.

561
00:38:53,891 --> 00:38:56,977
Isn't that a funny thing to think about?

562
00:38:56,978 --> 00:38:58,937
A lot of people, I think,

563
00:38:58,938 --> 00:39:02,273
they spot the one they love
from across the street sometimes.

564
00:39:02,274 --> 00:39:05,568
It's like they're running an
errand, and they happen to spot

565
00:39:05,569 --> 00:39:10,323
the one they love across the
street running a different errand.

566
00:39:10,324 --> 00:39:12,718
There's this sudden thrill, like...

567
00:39:13,327 --> 00:39:18,832
while the person you love is
inhabiting the same world as you.

568
00:39:18,833 --> 00:39:22,769
And they're just walking
around, buying, I don't know...

569
00:39:23,004 --> 00:39:25,036
Vitaminwater,

570
00:39:25,037 --> 00:39:27,465
and maybe they know that you
love them while they're doing it,

571
00:39:27,466 --> 00:39:31,820
and maybe it means
they're nice to a cashier.

572
00:39:33,055 --> 00:39:35,515
I never got to see her from
across the street, though.

573
00:39:35,516 --> 00:39:37,493
It just never happened.

574
00:39:38,144 --> 00:39:39,911
I don't want you to burn yourself.

575
00:39:41,605 --> 00:39:43,606
No burning, no.

576
00:39:45,777 --> 00:39:48,737
Rachel liked to smoke. She
said she was gonna stop,

577
00:39:48,738 --> 00:39:50,571
but she would have a
rule that she'd only smoke

578
00:39:50,572 --> 00:39:53,925
when we listened to this
one band we both loved.

579
00:39:55,202 --> 00:39:57,578
Because of the ceremony.

580
00:40:00,792 --> 00:40:05,229
"It should be a ceremony,"
it's what she said.

581
00:40:16,182 --> 00:40:20,536
Well, even if it's gum, I still
prefer to step outside to chew it.

582
00:40:21,187 --> 00:40:24,730
Like lighting a delicious cigarette.

583
00:40:24,731 --> 00:40:27,543
The things we miss, the rituals...

584
00:40:29,528 --> 00:40:31,338
Excuse me.

585
00:40:32,489 --> 00:40:35,259
- She doesn't like me.
- She will.

586
00:40:36,911 --> 00:40:38,703
Thanks for coming out
here and meeting with him.

587
00:40:38,704 --> 00:40:40,872
You know you can't legally
remove Claire Underwood,

588
00:40:40,873 --> 00:40:43,083
- even if you win in the House?
- I'm working on that.

589
00:40:43,084 --> 00:40:44,685
I'm not interested.

590
00:40:46,587 --> 00:40:50,756
He needs you. I need
you. So does the country.

591
00:40:50,757 --> 00:40:52,843
I'm not a politician.

592
00:40:52,844 --> 00:40:54,953
I can give orders, I can follow orders

593
00:40:54,954 --> 00:40:56,596
and I know when the hell to shut up.

594
00:40:56,597 --> 00:40:59,933
And you're a steady hand,
which, again, the country needs.

595
00:40:59,934 --> 00:41:03,561
- I'm not gonna be Conway's patsy.
- No, you're not.

596
00:41:03,562 --> 00:41:06,647
He's a difficult man to be around.

597
00:41:06,648 --> 00:41:09,901
It's over. It's over. The
process chose Claire Underwood.

598
00:41:09,902 --> 00:41:11,462
We'll get there.

599
00:41:13,739 --> 00:41:16,146
You'll be called vice president,

600
00:41:16,147 --> 00:41:20,329
but you will be, de
facto, something more.

601
00:41:28,754 --> 00:41:30,397
What were you doing there?

602
00:41:30,965 --> 00:41:34,425
I asked Seth Grayson
about Lisa Williams.

603
00:41:34,426 --> 00:41:37,678
Why the hell did you think that
was an appropriate thing to do?

604
00:41:37,679 --> 00:41:39,680
Didn't I tell you to drop this lead?

605
00:41:39,681 --> 00:41:41,641
Is it still a lead?
Because nobody's on it.

606
00:41:41,642 --> 00:41:43,977
Nobody's on it because she's a junkie,

607
00:41:43,978 --> 00:41:47,188
and everything she said to me is
in the Lucas Goodwin manifesto,

608
00:41:47,189 --> 00:41:48,356
which has been picked apart

609
00:41:48,357 --> 00:41:51,526
by every message board and
conspiracy theorist on the Internet.

610
00:41:51,527 --> 00:41:54,905
At first, Grayson said her
name didn't ring a bell,

611
00:41:54,906 --> 00:41:56,406
but then he called me out of the blue

612
00:41:56,407 --> 00:41:59,593
and asked me if we were still
looking into it. So I followed up.

613
00:42:04,540 --> 00:42:06,558
You looked through my notes.

614
00:42:13,090 --> 00:42:14,525
You're out.

615
00:42:15,968 --> 00:42:18,194
- What?
- You're fired, Jeffries.

616
00:42:19,096 --> 00:42:20,846
It's a story. I was following it.

617
00:42:20,847 --> 00:42:24,368
I don't want you on my
team. Get out of my office.

618
00:42:42,536 --> 00:42:46,831
We know we can win both states.
Why would we not take this deal?

619
00:42:46,832 --> 00:42:48,249
No. The Underwoods want a revote

620
00:42:48,250 --> 00:42:50,793
because they know they can't
win in the House. We can.

621
00:42:50,794 --> 00:42:53,671
We need to make a deal that
forces Claire Underwood to resign.

622
00:42:53,672 --> 00:42:56,466
- And why does that matter?
- If I may?

623
00:42:56,467 --> 00:42:58,384
- Madam Speaker.
- Speaking from the Hill,

624
00:42:58,385 --> 00:42:59,469
as vice president,

625
00:42:59,470 --> 00:43:01,846
she'll have a public pulpit
to discredit your every move.

626
00:43:01,847 --> 00:43:05,433
Oh, I'm going to exile Claire
Underwood to the Naval Observatory

627
00:43:05,434 --> 00:43:09,479
and stack her schedule with
state funerals in Southeast Asia.

628
00:43:09,480 --> 00:43:12,023
She'll never see the inside of
the West Wing, let alone the Oval.

629
00:43:12,024 --> 00:43:15,336
You need Brockhart by
your side, supporting you.

630
00:43:16,153 --> 00:43:17,612
I don't want to hear about Brockhart.

631
00:43:17,613 --> 00:43:19,905
What matters is that
Will becomes president.

632
00:43:19,906 --> 00:43:21,950
Underwood in office can cause damage.

633
00:43:21,951 --> 00:43:24,244
Whereas, if they lose, they're losers.

634
00:43:24,245 --> 00:43:28,623
I want this election to be
between me and Francis Underwood.

635
00:43:28,624 --> 00:43:29,958
Well, it isn't up to you.

636
00:43:29,959 --> 00:43:33,044
Speaker McCarthy isn't letting
this particular bill to the floor,

637
00:43:33,045 --> 00:43:36,047
unless we have an amendment attached
that removes Claire Underwood.

638
00:43:36,048 --> 00:43:37,441
Oh...

639
00:43:38,384 --> 00:43:39,675
Will...

640
00:43:39,676 --> 00:43:43,346
Now, if you expect a willing
partner in the White House,

641
00:43:43,347 --> 00:43:46,533
you'd better let that bill
get to the floor as is.

642
00:43:50,187 --> 00:43:51,625
If you get to the Oval, Will,

643
00:43:51,626 --> 00:43:54,024
you're gonna need the Speaker
more than she needs you.

644
00:43:54,025 --> 00:43:59,212
So, I'm gonna call her back,
and you are going to apologize.

645
00:44:04,035 --> 00:44:05,386
Will.

646
00:44:06,828 --> 00:44:10,098
Will, get back here!

647
00:44:11,458 --> 00:44:12,809
Will!

648
00:44:17,381 --> 00:44:18,732
Enough.

649
00:44:22,803 --> 00:44:24,279
Shut the door.

650
00:44:34,648 --> 00:44:36,500
You look like death.

651
00:44:37,443 --> 00:44:39,961
I can't get in touch
with the kidnappers.

652
00:44:41,863 --> 00:44:45,050
It's been radio silence
for almost 24 hours.

653
00:44:49,163 --> 00:44:51,932
There's rumors they don't
even have him anymore.

654
00:44:58,505 --> 00:45:01,650
I can't keep this from
the president any longer.

655
00:45:05,512 --> 00:45:06,947
Okay.

656
00:45:07,598 --> 00:45:10,701
And then I'll tell him
what a big help you've been.

657
00:45:16,148 --> 00:45:18,733
Sanctions hit in a couple of hours.

658
00:45:18,734 --> 00:45:23,571
Deal with Secretary Durant or with
some very unhappy billionaires.

659
00:45:23,572 --> 00:45:25,182
You will allow me to exploit

660
00:45:25,183 --> 00:45:27,867
whatever oil I find in Antarctica.

661
00:45:27,868 --> 00:45:31,429
And I'll tell you why. I have something.

662
00:45:31,663 --> 00:45:34,484
Or rather, someone you need.

663
00:45:34,485 --> 00:45:36,083
What are you talking about?

664
00:45:36,084 --> 00:45:37,752
Someone in your administration

665
00:45:37,753 --> 00:45:41,397
was willing to pay a great deal
of money for his safe return.

666
00:45:43,759 --> 00:45:48,012
Your Aidan Macallan.
He's here. In the Kremlin.

667
00:45:48,013 --> 00:45:51,307
Who do you think orchestrated that leak?

668
00:45:51,308 --> 00:45:53,476
Seems to know quite a lot.

669
00:45:53,477 --> 00:45:56,812
Excuse me one moment. The
Secretary of State is here.

670
00:45:56,813 --> 00:45:57,980
Madam Secretary.

671
00:45:57,981 --> 00:45:59,440
Gentlemen.

672
00:45:59,441 --> 00:46:03,211
- You must know Aidan?
- I don't believe I do.

673
00:46:07,115 --> 00:46:08,800
Nice to meet you.

674
00:46:09,701 --> 00:46:12,912
- You're American?
- I am.

675
00:46:12,913 --> 00:46:15,540
Before we begin, I'd
just like you to know

676
00:46:15,541 --> 00:46:20,729
that the president and the
former president are on the line.

677
00:46:21,297 --> 00:46:22,463
Cathy...

678
00:46:22,464 --> 00:46:24,900
You can pick up the phone, if you like.

679
00:46:29,054 --> 00:46:31,013
Mr. President.

680
00:46:31,014 --> 00:46:34,159
It's best you come back to Washington.

681
00:46:44,069 --> 00:46:45,546
Find anything yet?

682
00:46:46,488 --> 00:46:48,298
Not yet.

683
00:46:48,865 --> 00:46:52,177
Thank you again, Mr. Barnes,
for letting me take a look.

684
00:46:52,744 --> 00:46:54,095
I won't be long.

685
00:46:54,580 --> 00:46:56,372
This is everything?

686
00:46:56,373 --> 00:46:58,725
This is everything they
sent from Zoe's apartment.

687
00:47:01,253 --> 00:47:02,896
I just, you know...

688
00:47:04,298 --> 00:47:06,650
I just can't seem to throw anything out.

689
00:47:10,804 --> 00:47:13,615
I mean, it's just papers, but...

690
00:47:17,936 --> 00:47:20,521
Anyway, I'll leave you to it.

691
00:47:20,522 --> 00:47:23,458
- Okay. Thank you.
- Yeah.

692
00:48:21,917 --> 00:48:23,476
Okay.

693
00:48:27,130 --> 00:48:29,340
The Black Caucus? Really?

694
00:48:29,341 --> 00:48:32,217
I like Mark, because I
always know where he stands.

695
00:48:32,218 --> 00:48:33,928
It's usually on someone's neck.

696
00:48:33,929 --> 00:48:35,513
What did you buy
yourselves, another day?

697
00:48:35,514 --> 00:48:37,515
Enough time to talk to David Rasmussen,

698
00:48:37,516 --> 00:48:39,392
who you clearly spoke to before.

699
00:48:39,393 --> 00:48:40,956
And to meet with Jordan Sadowsky

700
00:48:40,957 --> 00:48:42,520
and the rest of the
Republican leadership.

701
00:48:42,521 --> 00:48:44,855
And what happens Tuesday? Or Wednesday?

702
00:48:44,856 --> 00:48:47,839
Look, I... I can have
David and the House

703
00:48:47,840 --> 00:48:49,234
vote on this every single session.

704
00:48:49,235 --> 00:48:50,563
Our backgrounds are different,

705
00:48:50,564 --> 00:48:52,738
but we're both
hardscrabble boys at heart.

706
00:48:52,739 --> 00:48:55,991
We need the last word,
last blow, last move.

707
00:48:55,992 --> 00:48:57,743
We can push the bill through the House

708
00:48:57,744 --> 00:49:00,413
for a new election for
president in Tennessee and Ohio.

709
00:49:00,414 --> 00:49:02,348
Speaker McCarthy won't let that happen.

710
00:49:02,749 --> 00:49:06,293
But I could... convince her

711
00:49:06,294 --> 00:49:08,128
if you were to step down,

712
00:49:08,129 --> 00:49:10,506
and our full tickets
president and vice president

713
00:49:10,507 --> 00:49:12,925
run against each other
in Tennessee and Ohio.

714
00:49:12,926 --> 00:49:15,821
Well, to hell with that.
Well, to hell with that.

715
00:49:16,972 --> 00:49:20,891
We'll make the deal. I think
this is the right thing to do.

716
00:49:20,892 --> 00:49:23,478
Tomorrow, I'll announce my intention
to step down after the revote.

717
00:49:23,479 --> 00:49:26,606
I'll let the Speaker know
to put the bill on the floor.

718
00:49:26,607 --> 00:49:28,774
We have one more outstanding
piece of business,

719
00:49:28,775 --> 00:49:30,568
the Supreme Court nominee.

720
00:49:30,569 --> 00:49:33,446
We'll need to appoint someone
to break the four-four deadlock.

721
00:49:33,447 --> 00:49:35,599
Approve Congress' new election date.

722
00:49:35,600 --> 00:49:37,729
We, uh, have several people in mind.

723
00:49:37,730 --> 00:49:40,062
I want you to appoint Elizabeth Benson.

724
00:49:42,247 --> 00:49:44,374
Well, she's liberal
enough for you to swallow,

725
00:49:44,375 --> 00:49:46,291
and conservative enough when it counts.

726
00:49:46,292 --> 00:49:48,252
We don't have time for Senate hearings.

727
00:49:48,253 --> 00:49:50,313
I can make a recess happen.

728
00:50:01,975 --> 00:50:04,119
I'll see you on the campaign trail.

729
00:50:13,612 --> 00:50:16,947
Where does that leave me, huh, Alex?

730
00:50:16,948 --> 00:50:19,324
I went out on a limb here.

731
00:50:19,325 --> 00:50:21,827
If we don't have a vote
in the House, then what?

732
00:50:21,828 --> 00:50:25,306
- Mark Usher is your friend, not mine.
- Mark Usher's no one's friend.

733
00:50:25,874 --> 00:50:28,709
Francis Underwood
hasn't won a thing, okay?

734
00:50:28,710 --> 00:50:31,253
And there's a good... more than
a good chance that Will Conway

735
00:50:31,254 --> 00:50:33,899
will be the next President
of the United States.

736
00:51:06,998 --> 00:51:11,186
Could you give me a moment?
It's been a difficult few days.

737
00:51:14,798 --> 00:51:17,049
Better?

738
00:51:17,050 --> 00:51:18,609
Yes.

739
00:51:20,178 --> 00:51:23,180
We couldn't let the House
vote proceed on Monday.

740
